[Line "Austrian Attack: 4...Nf6 5.Nf3 O-O 6.Bd3 Nc6 7.e5 dxe5 8.fxe5 Nh5"]
[Section "4.f4: the Austrian Attack"]
[Summary "The main line of the Pirc Austrian. White pushes e5 to cramp Black, Black takes twice and pins the f3-knight, aiming at the base of the e5-pawn with ...f6."]

1. e4 g6 2. d4 Bg7 3. Nc3 d6 4. f4 { The Austrian Attack, 16% of the elite games after 3...d6. White builds a pawn centre and prepares e5. The two plans
are an attack on the kingside (Nf3, Bd3, O-O, Qe1-h4) and a pawn push to e5 followed by a space advantage. } 4... Nf6 { [%cal Gf6e4]
The Pirc move order. Against 4.f4 the repertoire does not use the Tiger set-up as its first choice, because the knight on f6 pressures e4 at once and White has to make up his mind.
4...a6 is the second option (see the last game of this file). } 5. Nf3 { [%cal Gf3e5,Gf3g5] } ( 5. Bd3 O-O 6. Nf3 { Transposes. } )
5... O-O { Black castles: against the Austrian the king is safer than the centre, because White's pieces are not yet aimed at g7. The fianchetto bishop will become strong
once the e5 push has been dealt with. } 6. Bd3 { Supports e4 and prepares O-O. The other tries are 6.e5, 6.Be3 and 6.Qe1 (see the next games). }
6... Nc6 { [%cal Gc6d4,Ge7e5] The most popular reply: the knight puts a second attacker on d4 and prepares ...e5 or ...Bg4. 6...Na6 is the alternative, aimed at ...c5 and ...Nb4, and it answers e5 with ...Nd7. }
7. e5 { The most testing. White cramps Black and expects the knight on f6 to run away. } ( 7. O-O e5! { [%cal Ge7e5] White has castled, so Black
strikes in the centre. } 8. fxe5 dxe5 9. d5 Nd4 10. Nxe5 Nxe4 11. Nxe4 Bxe5 12. c3 Nf5 { The exchanges leave Black with a healthy game: the knight escapes to f5 and the bishop on e5 is strong. } )
( 7. d5 Nb4 8. Be2 c6 9. a3 Na6 10. Be3 { Black's knight is bounced around, but it lands on a6 and c5 is a good square; the pawn on c6 hits d5. } )
7... dxe5 { Taking is the main line: it opens the d-file and the diagonal of the bishop on g7. } 8. fxe5 { 8.dxe5 is also played; the resulting positions after ...Qxd1 are drawish. }
8... Nh5 { [%cal Gh5f4,Gf7f6] The knight steps off the attacked square. From h5 it covers f4 and g3, and it clears f6 for the pawn. } 9. Be3 Bg4 { [%cal Gg4f3,Gc6e5,Gg7e5] The pin on f3 makes e5 a real target: it is attacked by the
knight, by the bishop and soon by the f-pawn. } 10. Be2 { Breaks the pin. } 10... f6 { [%cal Gf6e5] The pawn undermines the base of the chain. After the exchange of the bishops on e2 and f3 Black
has a good game; the position is dynamic, and the book scores 46% for Black against 41% for White. } *

[Line "6.e5 dxe5 7.fxe5 Nd5 8.Bc4 Nxc3 9.bxc3 c5"]
[Section "4.f4: the Austrian Attack"]
[Summary "The Unzicker Attack. Black takes the pawn, hits the c3-knight and breaks the centre with ...c5."]

1. e4 g6 2. d4 Bg7 3. Nc3 d6 4. f4 Nf6 5. Nf3 O-O 6. e5 { The Unzicker Attack, 36% of the games here. The pawn attacks the knight at once. }
6... dxe5 { Taking. The other knight retreat gives White a lasting space advantage. }
( 6... Nfd7?! { Passive: the knight blocks the bishop and White keeps the space. } 7. h4 { A useful extra move, and White scores more than 55% in the elite database. } ) 7. fxe5 { [%cal Ge5f6] } ( 7. dxe5 Qxd1+ 8. Kxd1 Nh5 { [%cal Gh5f4] The knight hits f4 and the bishop hits e5, so White's centre is
the one under pressure; the queenless middlegame is equal. (After 8...Ng4 9.Ke1 White scores well in the book.) } )
7... Nd5 { [%cal Gd5c3,Gd5f4] } 8. Bc4 { Attacks the knight on d5 a second time, so Black has to decide what to do with it. } 8... Nxc3 { [%cal Gc3d1] The exchange doubles the white pawns and opens the b-file, and it is the main choice of the elite players. }
( 8... Be6?! { The line of one of the studies. } 9. Nxd5 Bxd5 10. Bxd5 Qxd5 11. Qe2 b5 { Playable, but the book shows White scoring better, and Black's queen has been drawn to d5 without
a concrete aim. } ) 9. bxc3 c5 { [%cal Gc5d4,Gg7e5] The break. White's pawn centre is lopsided: the pawn and the queen both hit d4, and once the e5-pawn is gone the bishop on g7 joins in. } 10. O-O Bg4 { [%cal Gg4f3,Gb8c6] The pin on f3 weakens the support of the e5-pawn, and ...Nc6 will pile up on it. Black has equal
chances and a flexible position; White has two bishops and a pawn centre but weaknesses on c3 and e5. } *

[Line "6.Be3 c6: the solid answer"]
[Section "4.f4: the Austrian Attack"]
[Summary "White develops with Be3 and Qd2. Black plays ...c6, ...Nbd7 and ...Nd5 and gets a very solid, drawish position."]

1. e4 g6 2. d4 Bg7 3. Nc3 d6 4. f4 Nf6 5. Nf3 O-O 6. Be3 { Prepares Qd2 and long castling. 6...Ng4 is available but the white bishop is happy to go to g1; the Kurajica line 6...b6
is critical but difficult for Black. } 6... c6 { [%cal Gc6d5,Gb8d7] The pawn prepares ...Nbd7 and ...Qa5, and takes d5 away from White's knight. }
( 6... b6?! { The Kurajica line. } 7. Qd2 Bb7 8. e5 Ng4 9. O-O-O c5 10. dxc5 bxc5 11. Bxc5 Qa5 12. Ba3 dxe5 13. Nd5 Qxd2+ 14. Rxd2 { White's pieces are more active and
Black's weaknesses on e7, c7 and b7 are hard to hold; the study's author thinks that Black can only hope to draw. } ) 7. h3 { Prevents ...Ng4 and ...Bg4. } 7... Nbd7 { [%cal Gd7b6,Gd7c5] } 8. e5 Nd5 { [%cal Gd5e3,Gd5c3] The knight attacks both the bishop on e3 and the knight on c3. }
9. Nxd5 cxd5 { Black has a healthy pawn on d5, which blocks the e5-pawn, and the open c-file. } 10. Bd3 Qb6 { [%cal Gb6b2,Gb6d4] The queen hits b2 and d4. White has to defend the pawn, and the position is exceptionally balanced: in the elite
database this line is drawn in about 85% of the games. A reasonable choice when a draw would do; the alternative is 6...Nc6 for a sharper game with more risks. } *

[Line "Second option: 5...c5 (the Dragon formation)"]
[Section "4.f4: the Austrian Attack"]
[Summary "Black strikes at once with ...c5. After dxc5 Qa5 the bishop on g7 has an open diagonal, and the forcing lines with Bb5+ lead to a draw or better."]

1. e4 g6 2. d4 Bg7 3. Nc3 d6 4. f4 Nf6 5. Nf3 c5 { [%cal Gc5d4,Gg7d4] The Dragon formation. It trades the c-pawn for White's d-pawn and removes the cramping chain at its base. In the elite database Black scores 44% against 45% for
White, better than after 5...O-O (40%), and it is the more active choice; its price is theory. } 6. dxc5 { [%cal Gc5d6] }
( 6. Bb5+ Bd7 { [%cal Gd7b5] } 7. e5 Ng4 8. e6 fxe6 9. Ng5 Bxb5 10. Nxe6 Bxd4 11. Nxd8 Bf2+ 12. Kd2 Be3+ 13. Ke1 Bf2+ { Black forces a draw by repetition. White can avoid it with 11.Nxb5 Qa5+, which is sharper. } )
( 6. Bb5+ Bd7 7. e5 Ng4 8. Bxd7+ Qxd7 9. d5 dxe5 10. h3 e4 11. Nxe4 Nf6 12. Nxf6+ Bxf6 13. O-O O-O { Black has the bishop pair against a weakened centre. } )
6... Qa5 { [%cal Ga5c5,Ga5c3] The queen hits the knight on c3 and the pawn on c5 will be taken back next move. } 7. Bd3 Qxc5 { [%cal Gc5c3] } 8. Qe2 O-O 9. Be3 { Hits the queen. } 9... Qa5 10. O-O Bg4 { [%cal Gg4f3,Gb8c6] Black pins the knight and brings the queenside knight out
to c6. White has better development; Black has the bishop pair's diagonal, a firm centre and the usual Dragon play. The book scores 49% for Black here against 34% for White. } *

[Line "Second option: 4...a6 (the Tiger against the Austrian)"]
[Section "4.f4: the Austrian Attack"]
[Summary "Black meets the Austrian with the same set-up as against the 150 Attack: ...a6, ...b5, ...Nd7 and ...c5, saving the king's knight for later."]

1. e4 g6 2. d4 Bg7 3. Nc3 d6 4. f4 a6 { [%cal Gb7b5] The Tiger's Modern against the f4 set-up. The knight stays on g8, so e5 attacks nothing, and ...b5 and ...c5 start the counterplay. } 5. Nf3 b5 { [%cal Gb5b4,Gc8b7] }
6. Bd3 Nd7 { [%cal Gd7c5,Gc7c5] The knight prepares ...c5 and keeps the bishop's diagonal open; 6...Bb7 is the other main choice and is more popular; 6...Nd7 keeps ...c5 in one move. } 7. O-O { White castles. }
( 7. e5 c5 8. Be4 Rb8 { The rook leaves the long diagonal. Black's pieces come out with ...Bb7 and ...Ngf6. } )
( 7. e5 c5 8. Ng5 Nh6 9. e6 Nf6 10. dxc5 dxc5 11. Be3 Bxe6 12. Bxc5 Nd5 13. Nxd5 Qxd5 14. Be3 Rc8 { A line from one of the studies. White has lost the e6-pawn, Black's pieces become active, and the white attack has run out of steam. } )
( 7. a4 b4 8. Ne2 Bb7 9. O-O c5 10. c3 Ngf6 { The same plan as against 4.Be3: ...b4 and ...c5. } )
7... c5 { [%cal Gc5d4] Strikes at the centre before White has found a plan. } 8. dxc5 Nxc5 { [%cal Gc5e4,Gg8f6,Gc8b7] The e4-pawn is attacked and the knight on c5 can't be hit by a pawn. White's pawns on e4 and f4 are now targets. Black has equalised: the next moves are ...Ngf6, ...Bb7 and ...O-O,
with pressure on e4. } *
